# Mutation cases for W3C SCXML 5.7.1 under 6.4 — what a `<param>` of an
# `<invoke>` costs when its expression will not evaluate.
#
# Run with `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/invoke_param_error_is_reported.cases`.
#
# Two clauses meet and only one governs. §scxml-6.4.2 terminates the element
# when "the evaluation of its arguments produces an error", and the sentence
# after it — "Otherwise the Processor MUST start a new logical instance" —
# makes the alternative explicit. §scxml-5.7.1 says a failing `<param>` costs
# `error.execution` AND the name and value, then delegates only the SUCCESSFUL
# name and value to the context, naming `<donedata>`, `<send>` and `<invoke>`
# in that sentence. So 5.7.1 has already answered, in this context by name,
# and 6.4.2 is the clause for the element's OWN arguments.
#
# No IRP document asks it here — test343 settles the same clause from the
# `<donedata>` side and nothing reaches the invoke context — which is why the
# seven channels held three different answers with every suite green: five
# silent (C++ AOT wrote a log line, which is not a queue), and Kotlin and the
# C++ Interpreter cancelling the whole invoke without saying so.
#
# The template reaches `cargo test` only through the committed tree the
# regeneration below writes, so both halves are declared: the generated file is
# compiled into the test, and `mutation_regen` is what carries a mutated
# template into it.
#
# Anchors are literal slices of the source; an edit that moves one is reported
# INCONCLUSIVE with the anchor quoted, never as a pass.

mutation_tests -p sce-rust-tests --test invoke_param_error_starts_the_child
mutation_targets tools/codegen/templates/rust/invoke_methods.rs.jinja2
# Every file the regeneration writes, not just the one the mutation reaches:
# snapshot and restore cover declared files only, and the round has to put the
# whole generated tree back or the case after it measures a moved baseline.
mutation_targets backends/rust/tests/src/integration/invoke_param_error_starts_the_child/invoke_param_error_starts_the_child_sm.rs
mutation_targets backends/rust/tests/src/integration/invoke_param_error_starts_the_child/invoke_param_error_starts_the_child__sce_synth_invoke__inv_probe_sm.rs
mutation_targets backends/rust/tests/src/integration/invoke_param_error_starts_the_child/mod.rs
mutation_regen scripts/regen_invoke_param_error_starts_the_child.sh

mutation_oracles backends/rust/tests/tests/invoke_param_error_starts_the_child.rs

# ── The failure goes unreported ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# The defect this fixture was written for, restored exactly: the parent
# evaluates, the evaluation fails, and nothing reaches the internal queue. The
# child still comes up, so the only thing missing is the sentence that would
# have explained the `<data>` it did not get.

mutation_case "a failed invoke param raises nothing" <<'PY'
edit("tools/codegen/templates/rust/invoke_methods.rs.jinja2",
     """                            engine.raise(sce_rust_runtime::EventWithMetadata::platform_error({{ machine_name }}Event::{{ 'error.execution' | to_event_variant }}, "<invoke> <param name='{{ param.name | escape_rust }}'> expr failed to evaluate"));""",
     """                            let _ = &e;""")
PY

# ── The failure takes the whole invoke with it ──────────────────────
#
# The other reading — §scxml-6.4.2 over 5.7.1 — which Kotlin and the C++
# Interpreter both held. The child never starts, so the document waits on a
# session that was never created: a stall rather than an error, which is what
# `failInvokeNotStarted` is there to name.

mutation_case "a failed invoke param cancels the child" <<'PY'
edit("tools/codegen/templates/rust/invoke_methods.rs.jinja2",
     """                        Err(e) => {""",
     """                        Err(e) => {
                            let _ = &e;
                            return;""")
PY

# ── The offending pair is seeded anyway ─────────────────────────────
#
# "MUST ignore the name and value" is the half a channel can drop while still
# looking well-behaved to a document that only watches the error queue: the
# child finds a placeholder under the failed name that the author never wrote.

mutation_case "the failed pair is seeded with a placeholder" <<'PY'
edit("tools/codegen/templates/rust/invoke_methods.rs.jinja2",
     """                            ::sce_rust_runtime::sce_log_error!("invoke param '{{ param.name | escape_rust }}' eval failed: {}", e);""",
     """                            ::sce_rust_runtime::sce_log_error!("invoke param '{{ param.name | escape_rust }}' eval failed: {}", e);
                            child_policy.set_param_value_in_script_engine("{{ param.name | escape_rust }}", ::sce_rust_runtime::ScriptValue::String(String::new()));""")
PY
